Z||000000||mark harmon||08-17-2000||01:47 PM||||I just bought a 86 SE that has the alt/oil pod gauge over radio. I've noticed that when rpm's are below 1300 the oil needle goes crazy. Above 1300 it works normal. The gauges have been replaced and I have an email from a guy that has the same problem and has replaced the sensor. Neither have fixed the problem. Anyone know of a fix or if this is something I have to live with?||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||08-17-2000||01:58 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">  the plug on the sending unit can get hot and brittle which will casue the wires to pull on their respective plug and the harmonics below 1300rpm are probly right to send it into a frenzy... check and make sure the plug is in good shape and make sure the ground to the guage is ok, if none of that.. try some dielectric grease may be its just a different kind of bad connection. might also want to check the motor mounts... the shaking of the motor with bad mounts can cause all kinds of weird things to act strange! ... just a couple ideas... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-18-2000||02:43 AM||||I added the Rally/aux. gauges to my '85 GT and I have the same problem.  It's common (unfortunately).  Welcome to the club (both the forum and the gauge problem).||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||08-18-2000||10:13 AM||||I think these oil gauges were made by some disco freak the way they Boogie around so much. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The only oil gauge that knows how to dance the night away HA HA.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/laugh2.gif">    ok I'm not so funny.||||reg||6
Z||000004||mark harmon||08-22-2000||01:38 PM||||I found this today:<P>Oil Pressure Sensor Filler Plug<BR>A common problem on A/C equipped V6 Fieros is the oil pressure gauge going berzerk. This is caused by water entering the oil pressure sensor through the vent hole in the plug. Installing a new sensor only cures the problem temporarily, because water will enter the plug again. To prevent this from occurring again GM recommends installing the oil pressure sensor filler plug P/N 1205 9168 in the hole that you see when you disconnect the plug from the sensor. This part costs less than a dollar. By the way, this problem does not occur on Fieros without A/C because the sensor is mounted horizontally on these vehicles, so water flows out of the vent hole and does not accumulate and enter the sensor. 85 GT's are also spared because the gauge is much slower than on Fieros with the extra gauge package. <BR>So the sensor goes bad on the 85's as well, you just don't notice it. <P>Oil Pressure Sensor Connector<BR>If you're too late and your oil pressure sensor connector has rotten away, the original GM part number for the 84-87 oil pressure sensor connector is 1208 5529. <P>||||reg||5
Z||000005||vinnywolfe||08-22-2000||08:39 PM||||i have a different problem with my oil pressure gauge. it seems to work fine when i'm driving (40-50 psi?). but at idle, the oil pressure light comes on intermittently as the needle dances above and below the red area.<BR>any ideas?<P>thanks,<BR>vinny<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000006||StuGood||08-23-2000||01:06 PM||||Well, in case it makes you feel better, my 1986 SE has the low(?) oil pressure problem, too.  Particularly when the weather's hot, at idle, sometimes the gage dips into the red area, and sometimes the idiot light comes on.  If I rev it slightly, the light will go off as the oil pressure comes up.<BR>Changing the oil didn't seem to help.<P>I intend to unscrew the oil pressure sending unit, apply various levels of air pressure to the fitting and see what the dash gage indicates.  Obviously, I'm <B>hoping</B> the gage is lying to me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> !  If it's telling the truth, at least I know that I've gotten 14 very enjoyable years out of this engine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !      <BR> ||||reg||3
Z||000007||Alex4mula||08-23-2000||03:46 PM||||When pluging the sensor I put some silicone around to seal itn so no water gets in. No problem since. First sensor died at about three years. Second sensor has 9 years now & going.||||reg||1
Z||000008||yobtah||08-23-2000||04:05 PM||||I have an '87 SE V6... I replaced my sender/switch today, but mine had a different problem from the water one.  My gauge had been very erratic... and I noticed that a small amount of oil was leaking from the bottom of the sender.  My sender was leaking around the ring seal on the bottom edge (the seam in the sender's outer housing).  Things seem better after the replacement... at least the gauge works correctly now.||||reg||1
